   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "**Headline Finding**: The unprecedented wave of new US liquefied natural gas (LNG) export capacity is expected to improve natural gas affordability and spur additional demand in price-sensitive Asian markets by 2030.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Supporting Data**: The United States is set to lead the next wave of LNG supply, with around 300 bcm/yr of new LNG export capacity expected to come online between 2024 and 2030 [Gas2025 p.45]. As Asian spot LNG prices move closer to the short-run marginal cost of US LNG supply between 2027 and 2030, this could spur additional gas demand, especially in price-sensitive Asian markets [Gas2025 p.7]. Price-elastic demand across the power sector, gas-intensive industry, and the transport sector could absorb an additional 65 bcm of LNG by 2030 [Gas2025 p.42].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages**: The growth of LNG supply is expected to enhance supply security and improve the affordability of natural gas, including in price-sensitive emerging import markets [Gas2025 p.5]. The expansion of natural gas infrastructure, especially in South and Southeast Asia, will be crucial to absorb additional LNG supply [Gas2025 p.42].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces**: A weaker macroeconomic environment, slower build-out of natural gas infrastructure, and contractual rigidities might limit the scope of the price-adjusted demand response [Gas2025 p.7]. If existing infrastructure in Southeast Asia and other emerging LNG-importing regions is not expanded, about one-quarter of the demand response may be at risk of not materializing [Gas2025 p.7].\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
