   "source": [
    "### Query 4:  How are the increasing frequency of negative wholesale electricity prices and the regulatory shift towards two-sided Contracts for Difference (CfDs) in Europe altering the revenue expectations and financial agility of developers investing in utility-scale solar PV?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 37,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "**Headline Finding:** The increasing frequency of negative wholesale electricity prices and the regulatory shift towards two-sided Contracts for Difference (CfDs) in Europe are altering the revenue expectations and financial agility of developers investing in utility-scale solar PV.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Supporting Data:** Two-sided CfDs are expected to trigger nearly half of Europe's policy-driven utility-scale solar PV and wind capacity additions through 2030 [Renewables2025 p.67]. However, the transition to market pricing introduces revenue volatility, affecting solar PV developers especially and raising financing costs, resulting in a 12% cutback in the utility-scale solar PV forecast [Renewables2025 p.25]. The occurrence of negative wholesale electricity markets continued to increase in many markets in 2025, signaling a lack of flexibility in the system [Electricity2026 p.111].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages:** The growing shares of wind and solar PV are transforming electricity markets, increasing integration challenges, and requiring a rapid increase in power system flexibility and grid investment [Renewables2025 p.8]. This shift is also driving changes in business models, with developers seeking to balance out and expand the value streams in their portfolios [Renewables2025 p.103].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces:** Greater forecasting uncertainty has led many developers to commit less capital in advance and keep their short-term investment options flexible, indicating a need for agility in response to changing market conditions [Renewables2025 p.103]. However, the excerpts do not provide information on specific countervailing forces that could mitigate the impact of negative wholesale electricity prices and two-sided CfDs on utility-scale solar PV developers.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
