         "1",
         "How is the unprecedented wave of new US liquefied natural gas (LNG) export capacity expected to impact natural gas affordability and spur additional demand in price-sensitive Asian markets by 2030?",
         "**Headline Finding**: The unprecedented wave of new US liquefied natural gas (LNG) export capacity is expected to improve natural gas affordability and spur additional demand in price-sensitive Asian markets by 2030.\n\n**Supporting Data**: The United States is set to lead the next wave of LNG supply, with around 300 bcm/yr of new LNG export capacity expected to come online between 2024 and 2030 [Gas2025 p.45]. As Asian spot LNG prices move closer to the short-run marginal cost of US LNG supply between 2027 and 2030, this could spur additional gas demand, especially in price-sensitive Asian markets [Gas2025 p.7]. Price-elastic demand across the power sector, gas-intensive industry, and the transport sector could absorb an additional 65 bcm of LNG by 2030 [Gas2025 p.42].\n\n**Cross-Sector Linkages**: The growth of LNG supply is expected to enhance supply security and improve the affordability of natural gas, including in price-sensitive emerging import markets [Gas2025 p.5]. The expansion of natural gas infrastructure, especially in South and Southeast Asia, will be crucial to absorb additional LNG supply [Gas2025 p.42].\n\n**Countervailing Forces**: A weaker macroeconomic environment, slower build-out of natural gas infrastructure, and contractual rigidities might limit the scope of the price-adjusted demand response [Gas2025 p.7]. If existing infrastructure in Southeast Asia and other emerging LNG-importing regions is not expanded, about one-quarter of the demand response may be at risk of not materializing [Gas2025 p.7].",
         "6"
        ],
        [
         "2",
         "How are the surge in US electricity demand and the 2025 federal emergency policy interventions collectively affecting the retirement schedules, capacity planning, and generation output of domestic coal-fired power plants?",
         "**Headline Finding**: The surge in US electricity demand and 2025 federal emergency policy interventions are collectively affecting the retirement schedules, capacity planning, and generation output of domestic coal-fired power plants by slowing down planned retirements and potentially sustaining coal availability longer than previously assumed.\n\n**Supporting Data**: \n- Planned retirements have slowed markedly, with latest reports of capacity to be retired in 2025 totaling 6.2 GW, well below earlier projections of 15 GW [Coal2025 p.30].\n- The policy environment has shifted in favor of coal in the near term, with executive actions reaffirming coal's role in national energy security and directing agencies to accelerate permitting and reduce regulatory barriers [Coal2025 p.29].\n- Federal policies, including executive orders and the DOI's leasing initiatives, could delay retirements and sustain coal availability longer than previously assumed [Coal2025 p.31].\n- Coal production in the United States is projected to be 473 Mt in 2025, driven by higher coal demand and policy actions [Coal2025 p.54].\n\n**Cross-Sector Linkages**: The surge in US electricity demand is driven by the continuing emergence of AI and its growing demand for electricity [Coal2025 p.55]. The policy interventions are also affecting the coal supply chain, with the DOI expanding leasing opportunities on federal lands and the DOE releasing additional acreage for new leasing [Coal2025 p.29].\n\n**Countervailing Forces**: The transformation of blast furnaces into production with a lower CO2 footprint may face economic hurdles, which could limit the impact of policy interventions on coal demand [Coal2025 p.31]. Additionally, global coal production is forecast to decline slightly through 2030, which could affect international markets and potentially counterbalance the effects of US policy interventions [Coal2025 p.8].",
         "6"
        ],
        [
         "3",
         "How are the increasing frequency of negative wholesale electricity prices and the regulatory shift towards two-sided Contracts for Difference (CfDs) in Europe altering the revenue expectations and financial agility of developers investing in utility-scale solar PV?",
         "**Headline Finding:** The increasing frequency of negative wholesale electricity prices and the regulatory shift towards two-sided Contracts for Difference (CfDs) in Europe are altering the revenue expectations and financial agility of developers investing in utility-scale solar PV.\n\n**Supporting Data:** Two-sided CfDs are expected to trigger nearly half of Europe's policy-driven utility-scale solar PV and wind capacity additions through 2030 [Renewables2025 p.67]. However, the transition to market pricing introduces revenue volatility, affecting solar PV developers especially and raising financing costs, resulting in a 12% cutback in the utility-scale solar PV forecast [Renewables2025 p.25]. The occurrence of negative wholesale electricity markets continued to increase in many markets in 2025, signaling a lack of flexibility in the system [Electricity2026 p.111].\n\n**Cross-Sector Linkages:** The growing shares of wind and solar PV are transforming electricity markets, increasing integration challenges, and requiring a rapid increase in power system flexibility and grid investment [Renewables2025 p.8]. This shift is also driving changes in business models, with developers seeking to balance out and expand the value streams in their portfolios [Renewables2025 p.103].\n\n**Countervailing Forces:** Greater forecasting uncertainty has led many developers to commit less capital in advance and keep their short-term investment options flexible, indicating a need for agility in response to changing market conditions [Renewables2025 p.103]. However, the excerpts do not provide information on specific countervailing forces that could mitigate the impact of negative wholesale electricity prices and two-sided CfDs on utility-scale solar PV developers.",
         "6"
        ],
        [
         "4",
         "How do the tax credit modifications under the US \"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\" (OBBBA) affect the investment economics of using domestic versus imported feedstocks for Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF), and what cascading impact will this biofuel transition have on the capacity rationalisation of traditional US West Coast refineries?",
         "**Headline Finding:** The US \"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\" (OBBBA) modifies tax credits to favor domestic feedstocks for Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF), making imported feedstocks less competitive.\n\n**Supporting Data:** The OBBBA reduces the 45Z tax credits by 43% for SAFs and eliminates tax credits for SAFs produced from imported feedstocks, except for those from Canada and Mexico [Renewables2025 p.160]. As a result, imported SAFs and SAFs made from imported feedstocks will receive almost 60% less total support, making them largely uncompetitive [Renewables2025 p.160].\n\n**Cross-Sector Linkages:** The shift towards domestic feedstocks for SAF production may lead to increased demand for domestic biofuels and feedstocks, potentially raising feedstock costs and putting pressure on margins [Renewables2025 p.148]. This, in turn, may impact the capacity rationalization of traditional US West Coast refineries as they adapt to changing market dynamics.\n\n**Countervailing Forces:** The proposed RFS updates and California's plans to strengthen Low Carbon Fuel Standard targets may maintain demand signals for biofuels, including SAF [Renewables2025 p.148]. However, uncertainty remains considerable due to the lack of final RFS rules and additional details on the 45Z tax credit [Renewables2025 p.148]. New trade developments, such as the revised trade agreement between the UK and the US, may also influence producer decisions [Renewables2025 p.148].",
         "6"
        ]
       ],
       "shape": {
        "columns": 3,
        "rows": 5
       }
